SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 302 Sales to Government Agencies and Exempt O
 rganizations (PDF)
DESCRIPTION:18 DEPARTMENT OF ADMINISTRATIVE AND FINANCIAL SERVICES \n \n12
 5 MAINE REVENUE SERVICES \n \nChapter 302: SALES TO GOVERNMENTAL AGENCIES 
 AND EXEMPT \nORGANIZATIONS\n \n \n \n \nSUMMARY:  Establishes administrati
 ve rules regarding tax-exempt sales and explains \nthe circumstances under
  which a retailer or service provider will be relieved of its burden \nof 
 proving that sales to an entity described in 36 M.R.S. §§ 1760 or 2557\,
  including an \nagency or instrumentality of the federal government or of 
 the State of Maine\, are exempt \nfrom Maine sales\, or use\, or service p
 rovider tax. \n \n  \nSECTION 1.  Exempt sales generally\; burden of proof
  \n 1. Generally Sales and Use Tax.  Sales to certain organizations and go
 vernmental \nagencies are exempt from sales and use tax pursuant to 36 M.R
 .S. § 1760.  Pursuant to 36 \nM.R.S. § 1760-C\, an exemption provided by
  36 M.R.S. § 1760 to a person based upon its \ncharitable\, nonprofit \, 
 or other public purposes applies only if the property or service \npurchas
 ed is intended to be used by the person primarily in the activity identifi
 ed by the \nparticular exemption.  The burden of proving a sale is to an o
 rganization that is exempt \nunder 36 M.R.S. § 1760 is on the person maki
 ng the sale.  The retailer is relieved of this \nburden of proof if it sat
 isfies the provisions of this rule.  \n 2. Certain purchases subject to ta
 x.  Pursuant to 36 M.R.S. § 1760- C\, an \nexemption provided by 36 M.R.S
 . § 1760 to a person based upon its charitable\, nonprofit\, \nor other p
 ublic purposes applies only if the property or taxable service purchased i
 s \nintended to be used by the person primarily in the activity identified
  by the particular \nexemption.  If property or taxable services that were
  purchased exempt are used primarily \nin an activity or  activities not i
 dentified by the particular exemption\, use tax liability \naccrues.   \nA
 n exemption based on charitable\, nonprofit\, or other public purposes  do
 es not \napply to any purchase for use by an exempt organization in it\nht
 tps://www.maine.gov/revenue/sites/maine.gov.revenue/files/inline-files/202
 6%20Rule%20302%20proposed_legmarkup.pdf
URL:https://www.maine.gov/revenue/sites/maine.gov.revenue/files/inline-fil
 es/2026%20Rule%20302%20proposed_legmarkup.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
